<div class="hero">
  <h1>I help busy, overscheduled teens raise their SAT and ACT scores — without adding one more stressful thing to their plate.</h1>
  <p class="lede">Most test prep treats a low score as a measure of intelligence. It isn't. Testing well is a skill, the same as any other skill — you can learn it, practice it, and get better at it.</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <p>For a lot of students, the score doesn't reflect what they actually know, because their nervous system is working against them the moment the timer starts. I work on both halves of that problem: the test itself, and the ability to stay calm enough under pressure to use what they've studied.</p>
  <p>I think of the SAT and ACT less as a verdict on who my students are, and more as one of the few real rites of passage left for teenagers today — something concrete to work toward and get better at, not something to fear.</p>
  <p>Alongside standard test-prep strategy, I draw on nervous-system regulation techniques from my HeartMath&reg; certification — a well-prepared student who freezes under pressure still won't show what they know.</p>
</div>

